    "result_text": "All done. The change is complete and the suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`. HTTP status codes are unchanged.\n\nAll 4xx rejections in this app funnel through escalation handlers under `src/main/resources/officefloor/escalation/` (functions throw checked exceptions; handlers respond). Some handlers already used Spring's `ProblemDetail` (e.g. `ValidationExceptionHandler`), but the 400 field-error handlers returned a bare `{\"errors\":[...]}` body and the 409/429 handlers returned an empty body \u2014 none of which is problem+json.\n\nI converted the nine offending handlers to build a `ProblemDetail` via the existing `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper (which sets `type=about:blank`, `title`, `status`, `detail`) and return it as `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>`. Spring's return-value handling then serializes it as `application/problem+json` automatically:\n\n- **429**: `DailyOwnerLimitExceptionHandler`\n- **409**: `DuplicateOwnerIdentityExceptionHandler`, `OwnerCityFullExceptionHandler`\n- **400**: `MissingOwnerFieldsExceptionHandler`, `InvalidEmailExceptionHandler`, `DisposableEmailDomainExceptionHandler`, `InvalidPostcodeExceptionHandler`, `InvalidTelephoneExceptionHandler`, `FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler`\n\nFor the 400 field handlers I preserved the offending-field information as an extra `errors` property on the problem body (RFC7807 permits extension members), so no diagnostic detail was lost.\n\nVerification: `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectionIsProblemJson` (a missing-`city` create \u2192 400) now passes its `application/problem+json` content-type and `$.status == 400` assertions, and the full test suite reports `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
